WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.286
Welcome to Lecture 8 of the

2
00:00:02.359 --> 00:00:04.860
FrontierCS 2 course, on databases.

3
00:00:05.210 --> 00:00:07.718
FrontierCS 2 takes a research paper

4
00:00:07.788 --> 00:00:09.530
removes its key idea from

5
00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:11.133
the authors' own code

6
00:00:11.133 --> 00:00:13.850
and asks an AI agent to write it back.

7
00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:16.839
To pass, it must beat the human reference

8
00:00:16.903 --> 00:00:18.384
on every measured case

9
00:00:18.384 --> 00:00:19.800
hidden ones included.

10
00:00:20.150 --> 00:00:22.597
This lecture has nine tasks

11
00:00:22.597 --> 00:00:24.870
all from papers in PVLDB.

12
00:00:25.220 --> 00:00:26.820
We start with joins.

13
00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:29.792
A database joins tables two at a time

14
00:00:29.855 --> 00:00:32.814
and a plan built that way can make intermediate

15
00:00:32.877 --> 00:00:35.080
results far larger than the answer.

16
00:00:35.430 --> 00:00:36.897
Take our small example

17
00:00:36.961 --> 00:00:38.683
shaped like the task page's

18
00:00:38.747 --> 00:00:40.150
Stack Overflow tables.

19
00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:43.060
Four users are left after a filter.

20
00:00:43.410 --> 00:00:45.529
There are a hundred twenty posts

21
00:00:45.595 --> 00:00:47.250
and six hundred comments.

22
00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:49.709
The binary plan joins comments

23
00:00:49.779 --> 00:00:52.240
with posts first: six hundred rows.

24
00:00:52.590 --> 00:00:55.550
Then it joins the users, and keeps sixty.

25
00:00:56.130 --> 00:00:57.883
The Yannakakis algorithm

26
00:00:57.956 --> 00:00:59.490
runs semijoins first.

27
00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:02.185
A semijoin keeps the rows of one table

28
00:01:02.247 --> 00:01:04.160
that have a partner in another.

29
00:01:04.510 --> 00:01:06.462
Posts keep only the fifteen

30
00:01:06.535 --> 00:01:08.270
owned by our four users.

31
00:01:08.620 --> 00:01:11.580
Comments keep only the sixty on those posts.

32
00:01:11.930 --> 00:01:13.680
Then it joins what is left

33
00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:15.041
the same sixty rows

34
00:01:15.041 --> 00:01:17.050
and nothing larger on the way.

35
00:01:17.630 --> 00:01:19.468
But the system converts only

36
00:01:19.534 --> 00:01:21.109
join trees of one shape

37
00:01:21.175 --> 00:01:23.341
every join's keys must sit in the

38
00:01:23.406 --> 00:01:25.310
leftmost table of both sides.

39
00:01:25.660 --> 00:01:27.337
Add votes to our example

40
00:01:27.404 --> 00:01:29.484
and the optimizer's plan breaks

41
00:01:29.551 --> 00:01:31.429
the rule at its middle join

42
00:01:31.429 --> 00:01:33.173
that join is on post ids

43
00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:34.917
but the leftmost table on

44
00:01:34.984 --> 00:01:36.460
its left is the users.

45
00:01:36.810 --> 00:01:38.576
The agent writes the rewrite

46
00:01:38.639 --> 00:01:40.090
that reshapes the tree.

47
00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:42.921
Claude Opus 5.5 saw that the joins

48
00:01:42.994 --> 00:01:45.037
link the tables into a tree

49
00:01:45.037 --> 00:01:47.080
with any table as its root.

50
00:01:47.430 --> 00:01:49.624
A left-deep spine from any root

51
00:01:49.624 --> 00:01:52.504
with each child's subplan joined onto it

52
00:01:52.504 --> 00:01:53.670
passes the rule.

53
00:01:54.020 --> 00:01:56.550
In our model, rooting at the six hundred

54
00:01:56.613 --> 00:01:57.941
comments is cheapest

55
00:01:57.941 --> 00:02:00.660
because the largest table is never hashed.

56
00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:03.640
The judge runs thirty-nine queries.

57
00:02:03.990 --> 00:02:05.665
The score is speed against

58
00:02:05.730 --> 00:02:07.340
the original binary plan

59
00:02:07.340 --> 00:02:09.273
but the pass rule is harsher

60
00:02:09.338 --> 00:02:11.335
every query must beat the human

61
00:02:11.399 --> 00:02:13.590
reference's median time, strictly.

62
00:02:13.940 --> 00:02:15.380
No model passed.

63
00:02:15.730 --> 00:02:17.300
Thirteen of sixteen scored

64
00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:18.568
above the reference

65
00:02:18.628 --> 00:02:20.560
but thirty-nine strict wins were

66
00:02:20.621 --> 00:02:22.130
too many for every model.

67
00:02:22.710 --> 00:02:24.390
JoinBoost, from 2023

68
00:02:24.390 --> 00:02:27.590
trains boosted trees inside a database

69
00:02:27.590 --> 00:02:30.150
and never builds the full join.

70
00:02:30.500 --> 00:02:33.351
After each tree, every sales row subtracts a

71
00:02:33.416 --> 00:02:35.100
share of its leaf's value

72
00:02:35.165 --> 00:02:37.239
and the leaf depends on features

73
00:02:37.303 --> 00:02:38.340
in other tables.

74
00:02:38.690 --> 00:02:41.782
The obvious way issues one update per leaf

75
00:02:41.782 --> 00:02:44.370
and each one scans every sales row.

76
00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:47.007
In our model of four hundred sales

77
00:02:47.007 --> 00:02:49.882
a tree of depth five has thirty-one leaves

78
00:02:49.948 --> 00:02:51.646
so that is twelve thousand

79
00:02:51.712 --> 00:02:53.280
four hundred row visits.

80
00:02:53.630 --> 00:02:55.597
One update with a case over the

81
00:02:55.660 --> 00:02:57.310
leaves needs four hundred.

82
00:02:57.890 --> 00:03:00.550
The task removes this residual update

83
00:03:00.550 --> 00:03:02.930
and ten of sixteen models passed.

84
00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:05.080
The best, Gemini 4 Argon

85
00:03:05.080 --> 00:03:06.808
running in Antigravity

86
00:03:06.808 --> 00:03:09.112
scored thirty-three point four

87
00:03:09.184 --> 00:03:11.128
about ten times faster than

88
00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:12.640
one update per leaf.

89
00:03:12.990 --> 00:03:16.510
Claude Opus 5.5 reorganized the data.

90
00:03:16.860 --> 00:03:19.672
It joined each branch of small tables once

91
00:03:19.737 --> 00:03:21.960
into a side table with every split

92
00:03:22.025 --> 00:03:23.660
test precomputed per key.

93
00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:26.292
So: drop the rows that cannot

94
00:03:26.363 --> 00:03:27.920
match before you join.

95
00:03:28.270 --> 00:03:30.263
Reshape a plan by choosing the

96
00:03:30.329 --> 00:03:31.790
root of its join tree.

97
00:03:32.140 --> 00:03:34.831
And update the residuals in one pass

98
00:03:34.831 --> 00:03:36.140
not one per leaf.

99
00:03:36.490 --> 00:03:37.466
As an exercise

100
00:03:37.466 --> 00:03:39.873
filter twenty users instead of four

101
00:03:39.873 --> 00:03:42.410
and see how the cheapest root changes.

102
00:03:42.760 --> 00:03:44.360
Next, FARGO.
